    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-003",
      "SourceSection": "8.2: Polynomial End Behavior",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sketch what you think the end behavior of your polynomial looks like, then check your work using graphing technology.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "8.2: Polynomial End Behavior",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Mai is studying the function \\(p(x) = \\text-\\frac{1}{100}x^3 + 25,\\!422x^2 + 8x + 26\\). She makes a table of values for \\(p\\) with \\(x = \\pm 1, \\pm 5, \\pm 10, \\pm 20\\) and thinks that this function has large positive output values in both…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider the polynomial \\(y=2x^5 - 5x^4 - 30x^3 + 5x^2 + 88x + 60\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Identify the degree of the polynomial.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Which of the 6 terms, \\(2x^5\\), \\(5x^4\\), \\(30x^3\\), \\(5x^2\\), \\(88x\\), or \\(60\\), is greatest when:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Describe the end behavior of the polynomial.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We know that if the expression for a polynomial function \\(f\\) written in factored form has the factor \\((x-a)\\), then \\(a\\) is a zero of \\(f\\) (that is,\\(f(a)=0\\)) and the point \\((a, 0)\\) is on the graph of the function. But what about…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The answer to this question depends on the degree of the polynomial, because any negative real number raised to an even power results in a positive number. For example, if we graph \\(y=x^2\\), \\(y=x^3\\) and \\(y=x^4\\) and zoom out, we see…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For both \\(y=x^2\\) and \\(y=x^4\\), large positive values of \\(x\\) or large negative values of \\(x\\) each result in large positive values of \\(y\\). But for \\(y=x^3\\), large positive values of \\(x\\) result in large positive values of \\(y\\),…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider the polynomial \\(P(x)=x^4-30x^3-20x^2+1000\\). The leading term, \\(x^4\\), almost seems smaller than the other 3 terms. For certain values of \\(x\\), this is even true. But, for values of \\(x\\) far away from zero, the leading term…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The value of the leading term \\(x^4\\) determines the end behavior of the function, that is, how the outputs of the function change as we look at input values farther and farther from 0. In the case of \\(P(x)\\), as \\(x\\) gets larger and…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How the outputs of a function change as we look at input values further and further from 0.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l08.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L08-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "8.3: Two Polynomial Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This function shows different end behavior in the positive and negative directions. In the positive direction the values get larger and larger. In the negative direction the values get closer and closer to -3.",
